1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:06,599
Appendix THE PRINCIPLES OF NEWSPEAK Newspeak was the official language of

2
00:00:06,599 --> 00:00:13,500
Oceania and had been devised to meet the ideological needs of intock or English Socialism.

3
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,180
In the year 1984 there was not as yet anyone who used news speakers his sole

4
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:21,779
means of communication, either in speech or writing.

5
00:00:21,779 --> 00:00:25,439
The leading articles in the Times were written in it but this was a tour de

6
00:00:25,439 --> 00:00:28,317
force which could only be carried out by a specialist.

7
00:00:28,317 --> 00:00:32,878
It was expected that Newspeak would have finally superseded old speak or Standard

8
00:00:32,878 --> 00:00:36,540
English, as we should call it) by about the year 2050..

9
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:40,977
Meanwhile it gained ground steadily, all Party members tending to use news-speak

10
00:00:40,977 --> 00:00:45,180
words and grammatical constructions more and more in their everyday speech.

11
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,977
The version in use in 1984, and embodied in the 9th and 10th Editions of the

12
00:00:49,977 --> 00:00:54,419
Newspeak Dictionary, was a provisional one, and contained many superfluous words

13
00:00:54,419 --> 00:00:58,137
and archaic formations which were due to be suppressed later.

14
00:00:58,137 --> 00:01:02,099
It is with the final, perfected version, as embodied in the 11th Edition of the

15
00:01:02,099 --> 00:01:05,580
Dictionary, that we are concerned here.

16
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:09,240
The purpose of Newspeak was not only to provide a medium of expression for the

17
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:13,137
world view and mental habits proper to the devotees of insock but to make all

18
00:01:13,137 --> 00:01:15,119
other modes of thought impossible.

19
00:01:15,119 --> 00:01:19,080
It was intended that when Newspeak had been adopted once and for all and all

20
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:22,860
speak forgotten, a heretical thought— —that is, a thought diverging from the

21
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:26,878
principles of insock should be literally unthinkable, at least so far as thought

22
00:01:26,878 --> 00:01:28,860
is dependent on words.

23
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:32,817
Its vocabulary was so constructed as to give exact and often very subtle

24
00:01:32,817 --> 00:01:36,200
expression to every meaning that a Party member could properly wish to express,

25
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,378
while excluding all other meanings and also the possibility of arriving at them

26
00:01:40,378 --> 00:01:42,058
by indirect methods.

27
00:01:42,058 --> 00:01:46,200
This was done partly by the invention of new words, but chiefly by eliminating

28
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,977
undesirable words and by stripping such words as remained of unorthodox meanings,

29
00:01:49,977 --> 00:01:53,817
and so far as possible of all secondary meanings whatever.

30
00:01:53,817 --> 00:01:58,439
To give a single example. The word free still existed in Newspeak, but it could

31
00:01:58,439 --> 00:02:02,939
only be used in such statements as 'This dog is free from lice' or 'This field is

32
00:02:02,939 --> 00:02:04,500
free from weeds'.

33
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:08,840
It could not be used in its old sense of 'politically free' or 'intellectually free',

34
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:13,860
since political and intellectual freedom no longer existed even as concepts, and

35
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:16,558
were therefore of necessity nameless.

36
00:02:16,558 --> 00:02:20,398
Quite apart from the suppression of definitely heretical words, reduction of

37
00:02:20,398 --> 00:02:24,240
vocabulary was regarded as an end in itself there's no word that could be

38
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:27,080
dispensed with was allowed to survive.

39
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:32,099
Newspeak was designed not to extend but to diminish the range of thought, and

40
00:02:32,099 --> 00:02:37,317
this purpose was indirectly assisted by cutting the choice of words down to a minimum.

41
00:02:37,317 --> 00:02:41,040
Newspeak was founded on the English language as we now know it, though many

42
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:45,297
new speak sentences, even when not containing newly created words, would be

43
00:02:45,297 --> 00:02:48,898
barely intelligible to an English speaker of our own day.

44
00:02:48,898 --> 00:02:53,040
Newspeak words were divided into three distinct classes, known as the A

45
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:58,800
vocabulary, the B vocabulary (also called compound words), and the C vocabulary.

46
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:02,519
It will be simpler to discuss each class separately, but the grammatical

47
00:03:02,519 --> 00:03:05,758
peculiarities of the language can be dealt with in that section devoted to

48
00:03:05,758 --> 00:03:11,660
the A vocabulary, since the same rules held good for all three categories.

49
00:03:11,699 --> 00:03:13,917
The A vocabulary.

50
00:03:13,917 --> 00:03:17,819
The A vocabulary consisted of the words needed for the business of everyday life—

51
00:03:17,819 --> 00:03:22,860
—for such things as eating, drinking, working, putting on one's clothes, going

52
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:27,480
up and down stairs, riding in vehicles, gardening, cooking, and the like.

53
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:31,680
It was composed almost entirely of words that we already possess words like hit,

54
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:37,139
run, dog, tree, sugar, house, field— —but in comparison with the present-day

55
00:03:37,139 --> 00:03:40,860
English vocabulary the number was extremely small, while their meanings

56
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:44,819
were far more rigidly defined. All ambiguities and shades of meaning had

57
00:03:44,819 --> 00:03:46,500
been purged out of them.

58
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,339
So far as it could be achieved, a new speak word of this class was simply a

59
00:03:50,339 --> 00:03:54,057
staccato sound expressing one clearly understood concept.

60
00:03:54,057 --> 00:03:57,417
It would have been quite impossible to use the A vocabulary for literary

61
00:03:57,417 --> 00:04:00,781
purposes or for political or philosophical discussion.

62
00:04:00,781 --> 00:04:04,622
It was intended only to express simple, purposive thoughts,

63
00:04:04,622 --> 00:04:09,180
usually involving concrete objects or physical actions.

64
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:13,557
The grammar of Newspeak had two outstanding peculiarities. The first of

65
00:04:13,557 --> 00:04:17,877
these was an almost complete interchangeability between different parts of speech.

66
00:04:17,877 --> 00:04:22,438
Any word in the language (in principle is applied even to very abstract words such

67
00:04:22,438 --> 00:04:28,019
as if or when) could be used either as a verb, noun, adjective or adverb.

68
00:04:28,019 --> 00:04:32,160
Between the verb and the noun form, when they were of the same root, there was

69
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:37,377
never any variation, this rule of itself involving the destruction of many archaic forms.

70
00:04:37,377 --> 00:04:42,360
The word thought, for example, did not exist in Newspeak. Its place was taken by

71
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:46,199
think, which did duty for both noun and verb.

72
00:04:46,199 --> 00:04:50,281
No etymological principle was followed here: in some cases it was the original

73
00:04:50,281 --> 00:04:54,781
noun that was chosen for retention, in other cases the verb. Even where a noun

74
00:04:54,781 --> 00:04:58,737
and verb of kindred meaning were not etymologically connected, one or other of

75
00:04:58,737 --> 00:05:00,781
the most frequently suppressed.

76
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:04,680
There was, for example, no such word as cut, its meaning being sufficiently

77
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:06,781
covered by the noun verb knife.

78
00:05:06,781 --> 00:05:13,199
Adjectives were formed by adding the suffix full to the noun verb and adverbs by adding -wise.

79
00:05:13,199 --> 00:05:18,000
Thus, for example, speedful meant 'rapid' and speed wise meant 'quickly'.

80
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,160
Certain of our present-to-day adjectives, such as good, strong, big, black, soft, were

81
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:25,980
retained, but their total number was very small.

82
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:29,578
There was little need for them, since almost any adjectival meaning could be

83
00:05:29,578 --> 00:05:32,459
arrived at by adding full to a noun- verb.

84
00:05:32,459 --> 00:05:38,639
None of the now existing adverbs was retained, except for a very few ending in -wise: the

85
00:05:38,639 --> 00:05:40,917
-wise determination was invariable.

86
00:05:40,917 --> 00:05:48,540
The word well, for example, was replaced by good wise In addition, any word— —this again applied

87
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:53,699
in principle to every word in the language— —could be negative by adding the FX on

88
00:05:53,699 --> 00:05:59,519
or could be strengthened by the affix plus-, or, for still greater emphasis double Plus

89
00:05:59,519 --> 00:06:04,622
Thus, for example, uncold meant 'warm', while Plus Cold under double Plus Cold

90
00:06:04,622 --> 00:06:08,938
meant, respectively, 'very cold' and 'superlatively cold'.

91
00:06:08,938 --> 00:06:12,959
It was also possible, as in present-day English, to modify the meaning of almost

92
00:06:12,959 --> 00:06:20,040
any word by prepositional affixes such as anti-post up-, down-, etc. By such methods

93
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:24,122
it was found possible to bring about an enormous diminution of vocabulary.

94
00:06:24,122 --> 00:06:29,040
Given, for instance, the word good, there was no need for such a word as bad, since

95
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:33,360
the required meaning was equally well— —indeed, better— —expressed by ungood.

96
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:36,781
All that was necessary, in any case where two words formed a natural pair of

97
00:06:36,781 --> 00:06:40,078
opposites, was to decide which of them to suppress.

98
00:06:40,078 --> 00:06:45,898
Dark, for example, could be replaced by unlight, or light by undark, according to preference.

99
00:06:45,898 --> 00:06:50,339
The second distinguishing mark of Newspeak grammar was its regularity.

100
00:06:50,339 --> 00:06:55,800
Subject to a few exceptions which are mentioned below, all inflections followed the same rules.

101
00:06:55,800 --> 00:07:00,959
Thus, in all verbs the preterite and the past participle were the same and ended in -ed.

102
00:07:00,959 --> 00:07:06,000
The predator of Steel was steeled the preterative think was thinked, and so on

103
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,917
throughout the language, all such forms of swam, gave, brought, spoke, taken, etc.,

104
00:07:10,917 --> 00:07:17,160
being abolished. All plurals were made by adding s or -es as the case might be.

105
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:24,540
The plurals of man, ox, life, were man's ox's lives Comparison of adjectives was invariably

106
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:30,660
made by adding er, -est (good goodest), irregular forms and the

107
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:33,898
more, most formation being suppressed.

108
00:07:33,898 --> 00:07:37,438
The only classes of words that were still allowed to inflict irregularly

109
00:07:37,438 --> 00:07:43,139
were the pronouns, the relatives, the demonstrative adjectives and the auxiliary verbs.

110
00:07:43,139 --> 00:07:47,519
All of these followed their ancient usage, except that whom had been scrapped

111
00:07:47,519 --> 00:07:52,258
as unnecessary, and the shell showed tenses had been dropped, all their uses

112
00:07:52,258 --> 00:07:56,758
being covered by will and wood There were also certain irregularities

113
00:07:56,758 --> 00:08:00,898
in word formation arising out of the need for rapid and easy speech. A word

114
00:08:00,898 --> 00:08:04,800
which was difficult to utter, or was liable to be incorrectly heard, was held

115
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:09,000
to be obso fact of a bad word: occasionally therefore, for the sake of

116
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,819
euphony, extra letters were inserted into a word or an archaic formation was retained.

117
00:08:14,819 --> 00:08:19,438
But this need made itself felt chiefly in connection with the B vocabulary.

118
00:08:19,438 --> 00:08:23,218
Why so great an importance was attached to ease of pronunciation will be made

119
00:08:23,218 --> 00:08:26,480
clear later in this essay.

120
00:08:26,639 --> 00:08:28,622
The B vocabulary.

121
00:08:28,622 --> 00:08:32,281
The B vocabulary consisted of words which had been deliberately constructed

122
00:08:32,281 --> 00:08:36,300
for political purposes: words, that is to say, which not only had

123
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:40,622
in every case a political implication, but were intended to impose a desirable

124
00:08:40,622 --> 00:08:43,139
mental attitude upon the person using them.

125
00:08:43,139 --> 00:08:46,980
Without a full understanding of the principles of insock it was difficult to

126
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:48,839
use these words correctly.

127
00:08:48,839 --> 00:08:52,917
In some cases they could be translated into Old speak or even into words taken

128
00:08:52,917 --> 00:08:57,122
from the A vocabulary, but this usually demanded a long paraphrase and always

129
00:08:57,122 --> 00:09:00,180
involved the loss of certain overtones.

130
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:04,139
The B words were a sort of verbal shorthand, often packing whole ranges of

131
00:09:04,139 --> 00:09:07,860
ideas into a few syllables, and at the same time more accurate and forcible

132
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:10,622
than ordinary language.

133
00:09:10,622 --> 00:09:15,597
The B words were in all cases compound words compound words such as speak right were

134
00:09:15,597 --> 00:09:18,781
of course to be found in the a vocabulary but these were merely

135
00:09:18,781 --> 00:09:22,800
convenient abbreviations and had no special ideological color

136
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:27,300
They consisted of two or more words, or portions of words, welded together in an

137
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:29,519
easily pronounceable form.

138
00:09:29,519 --> 00:09:35,339
The resulting amalgam was always a noun verb and inflected according to the ordinary rules.

139
00:09:35,339 --> 00:09:40,557
To take a single example: the word good think meaning, very roughly, 'orthodoxy', or,

140
00:09:40,557 --> 00:09:44,578
if one chose to regard it as a verb, 'to think in an orthodox manner'.

141
00:09:44,578 --> 00:09:51,360
This inflected as follows non-verb good think past tense and past participle good things

142
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:56,519
present participle good thinking adjective good thinkful adverb good

143
00:09:56,519 --> 00:10:04,737
think wise verbal noun good thinker The B words were not constructed on any etymological plan.

144
00:10:04,737 --> 00:10:08,398
The words of which they were made up could be any parts of speech, and could

145
00:10:08,398 --> 00:10:11,877
be placed in any order and mutilated in any way which made them easy to

146
00:10:11,877 --> 00:10:14,519
pronounce while indicating their derivation.

147
00:10:14,519 --> 00:10:18,980
In the word crime think thought crime for instance, the think came second,

148
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:24,237
whereas in thinkpole Thought Police) it came first, and in the latter word police

149
00:10:24,237 --> 00:10:26,398
had lost its second syllable.

150
00:10:26,398 --> 00:10:30,360
Because of the great difficulty in securing euphony, irregular formations

151
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:34,019
were commoner in the B vocabulary than in the A vocabulary.

152
00:10:34,019 --> 00:10:38,040
For example, the adjective forms of miniature mini packs and mini love were,

153
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:43,737
respectively many truthful many peaceful and many lovely simply because truthful

154
00:10:43,737 --> 00:10:47,459
pactful and -loveful were slightly awkward to pronounce.

155
00:10:47,459 --> 00:10:53,938
In principle, however, all B words could inflict and all inflected in exactly the same way.

156
00:10:53,938 --> 00:10:57,839
Some of the B words had highly subtleized meanings, barely intelligible

157
00:10:57,839 --> 00:11:00,898
to anyone who had not mastered the language as a whole.

158
00:11:00,898 --> 00:11:04,680
Consider, for example, such a typical sentence from the Times leading article

159
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:09,781
as old thinkers unbelief feelingsok The shortest rendering that one could make

160
00:11:09,781 --> 00:11:13,377
of this in Old speak would be: 'Those whose ideas were formed before the

161
00:11:13,377 --> 00:11:17,877
Revolution cannot have a full emotional understanding of the principles of English Socialism.'

162
00:11:17,877 --> 00:11:19,980
But this is not an adequate translation.

163
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:24,122
To begin with, in order to grasp the full meaning of the new speak sentence quoted

164
00:11:24,122 --> 00:11:28,680
above, one would have to have a clearer idea of what is meant by yingsok

165
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:32,339
And in addition, only a person thoroughly grounded in ingstock could appreciate

166
00:11:32,339 --> 00:11:36,597
the full force of the word belly feel which implied a blind, enthusiastic

167
00:11:36,597 --> 00:11:41,160
acceptance difficult to imagine today; or of the word old zinc which was

168
00:11:41,160 --> 00:11:45,180
inextricably mixed up with the idea of wickedness and decadence.

169
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:48,597
But the special function of certain Newspeak words, of which old think was

170
00:11:48,597 --> 00:11:53,097
one, was not so much to express meanings as to destroy them.

171
00:11:53,097 --> 00:11:57,000
These words, necessarily few in number, had had their meanings extended until

172
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,718
they're contained within themselves whole batteries of words which, as they

173
00:12:00,718 --> 00:12:05,398
were sufficiently covered by a single comprehensive term, could now be scrapped and forgotten.

174
00:12:05,398 --> 00:12:08,758
The greatest difficulty facing the compilers of the new speak Dictionary

175
00:12:08,758 --> 00:12:13,078
was not to invent new words, but, having invented them, to make sure what they

176
00:12:13,078 --> 00:12:19,578
meant: to make sure, that is to say, what ranges of words they canceled by their existence.

177
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:24,057
As we have already seen in the case of the word free, words which had once borne

178
00:12:24,057 --> 00:12:27,540
a heretical meaning were sometimes retained for the sake of convenience, but

179
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:32,519
only with the undesirable meanings purged out of them count as other words such as honor

180
00:12:32,519 --> 00:12:39,360
justice, morality, internationalism, democracy, science and religion had simply ceased to exist.

181
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:43,557
A few blanket words covered them, and, in covering them, abolished them.

182
00:12:43,557 --> 00:12:47,097
All words grouping themselves around the concepts of liberty and equality, for

183
00:12:47,097 --> 00:12:51,417
instance, were contained in the single word crimethink, while all words grouping

184
00:12:51,417 --> 00:12:54,417
themselves around the concepts of objectivity and rationalism were

185
00:12:54,417 --> 00:12:58,500
contained in the single word old think Greater precision would have been

186
00:12:58,500 --> 00:13:02,938
dangerous. What was required in a Party member was an outlook similar to that of

187
00:13:02,938 --> 00:13:06,540
the ancient Hebrew who knew, without knowing much else, that all nations other

188
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:10,737
than his own worshiped false gods'. He did not need to know that these gods were

189
00:13:10,737 --> 00:13:16,917
called Bal Osiris molok asteroth and the like: probably the less he knew about

190
00:13:16,917 --> 00:13:19,139
them the better for his orthodoxy.

191
00:13:19,139 --> 00:13:23,339
He knew Jehovah and the commandments of Jehovah: he knew, therefore, that all gods

192
00:13:23,339 --> 00:13:27,300
with other names or other attributes were false gods. In somewhat the same way,

193
00:13:27,300 --> 00:13:32,819
the Party member knew what constituted right conduct, and in exceedingly vague generalized

194
00:13:32,819 --> 00:13:36,417
terms he knew what kinds of departure from it were possible.

195
00:13:36,417 --> 00:13:40,557
His sexual life, for example, was entirely regulated by the two news-speak words

196
00:13:40,557 --> 00:13:48,237
sex crime sexual immorality) and good sex chastity sex crime covered all sexual misdeeds

197
00:13:48,237 --> 00:13:53,519
whatever. It covered fornication, adultery, homosexuality and other perversions, and,

198
00:13:53,519 --> 00:13:56,758
in addition, normal intercourse practiced for its own sake.

199
00:13:56,758 --> 00:14:00,360
There was no need to enumerate them separately, since they were all equally

200
00:14:00,360 --> 00:14:04,258
culpable, and, in principle, all punishable by death.

201
00:14:04,258 --> 00:14:08,699
In the C vocabulary, which consisted of scientific and technical words, it might

202
00:14:08,699 --> 00:14:12,959
be necessary to give specialized names to certain sexual aberrations, but the

203
00:14:12,959 --> 00:14:15,417
ordinary citizen had no need of them.

204
00:14:15,417 --> 00:14:19,622
He knew what was meant by good sex that is to say, normal intercourse between man

205
00:14:19,622 --> 00:14:23,160
and wife, for the sole purpose of begetting children, and without physical

206
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:27,480
pleasure on the part of the woman: all else was sex crime

207
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:31,557
In Newspeak it was seldom possible to follow a heretical thought further than

208
00:14:31,557 --> 00:14:37,680
the perception that it was heretical: beyond that point the necessary words were non-existent.

209
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:41,459
No word in the B vocabulary was ideologically neutral.

210
00:14:41,459 --> 00:14:43,737
A great many were euphemisms.

211
00:14:43,737 --> 00:14:48,480
Such words, for instance, as joycamp forced labor camp) or mini packs Ministry

212
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:53,578
of Peace that is Ministry of War) meant almost the exact opposite of what they appeared to mean.

213
00:14:53,578 --> 00:14:57,360
Some words, on the other hand, displayed a frank and contemptuous understanding of

214
00:14:57,360 --> 00:14:59,578
the real nature of Oceanic society.

215
00:14:59,578 --> 00:15:03,718
An example with Pro feed meaning the rubbishy entertainment at spurious news

216
00:15:03,718 --> 00:15:06,898
which the Party handed out to the masses.

217
00:15:06,898 --> 00:15:10,917
Other words, again, were ambivalent, having the connotation Goodwin applied to the

218
00:15:10,917 --> 00:15:13,860
Party and 'bad' when applied to its enemies.

219
00:15:13,860 --> 00:15:17,218
But in addition there were great numbers of words which at first sight appeared

220
00:15:17,218 --> 00:15:20,758
to be mere abbreviations and which derived their ideological color not from

221
00:15:20,758 --> 00:15:24,557
their meaning but from their structure.

222
00:15:24,898 --> 00:15:27,781
So far as it could be contrived, everything that had or might have

223
00:15:27,781 --> 00:15:32,097
political significance of any kind was fitted into the B vocabulary.

224
00:15:32,097 --> 00:15:36,540
The name of every organization or body of people, or doctrine, or country, or

225
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:40,557
institution, or public building, was invariably cut down into the familiar

226
00:15:40,557 --> 00:15:45,237
shape; that is, a single easily pronounced word with the smallest number of

227
00:15:45,237 --> 00:15:48,300
syllables that would preserve the original derivation.

228
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:52,139
In the Ministry of Truth, for example, the Records Department, in which Winston

229
00:15:52,139 --> 00:15:56,639
Smith worked, was called Rick Depp the Fiction Department was called fikodep

230
00:15:56,639 --> 00:16:01,199
the teleprograms Department was called Teledep, and so on.

231
00:16:01,199 --> 00:16:04,256
This was not done solely with the object of saving time.

232
00:16:04,256 --> 00:16:08,459
Even in the early decades of the 20th century, telescoped words and phrases had

233
00:16:08,459 --> 00:16:12,120
been one of the characteristic features of political language; and it had been

234
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:15,360
noticed that the tendency to use abbreviations of this kind was most

235
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:19,620
marked in totalitarian countries and totalitarian organizations

236
00:16:19,620 --> 00:16:26,819
Examples were such words as Nazi, Gestapo come in turn in Precor hajit prop

237
00:16:26,819 --> 00:16:30,660
In the beginning the practice had been adopted as it were instinctively, but in

238
00:16:30,660 --> 00:16:34,739
Newspeak it was used with a conscious purpose. It was perceived that in thus

239
00:16:34,739 --> 00:16:38,400
abbreviating a name one narrowed and subtly altered its meaning, by cutting

240
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:41,519
out most of the associations that would otherwise cling to it.

241
00:16:41,519 --> 00:16:45,480
The words Communist International, for instance called up a composite picture

242
00:16:45,480 --> 00:16:50,160
of universal human brotherhood, red flags, barricades, Karl Marx and the Paris

243
00:16:50,160 --> 00:16:55,080
Commune. The word commentone on the other hand, suggests merely a tightly knit

244
00:16:55,080 --> 00:17:00,120
organization and a well-defined body of doctrine refers to something almost as easily

245
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:04,139
recognized and as limited in purpose, as a chair or a table

246
00:17:04,139 --> 00:17:07,279
come in turn is a word that can be uttered almost without taking thought,

247
00:17:07,279 --> 00:17:11,095
whereas Communist International is a phrase over which one is obliged to

248
00:17:11,095 --> 00:17:13,319
linger at least momentarily.

249
00:17:13,319 --> 00:17:18,000
In the same way, the association's called up by a word like miniature were fewer

250
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:21,415
and more controllable than those called up by Ministry of Truth.

251
00:17:21,415 --> 00:17:25,199
This accounted not only for the habit of abbreviating whenever possible,

252
00:17:25,199 --> 00:17:31,756
but also for the almost exaggerated care that was taken to make every word easily pronounceable.

253
00:17:31,860 --> 00:17:37,019
In Newspeak, euphony outweighed every consideration other than exactitude of meaning.

254
00:17:37,019 --> 00:17:41,459
Regularity of grammar was always sacrificed to it when it seemed necessary.

255
00:17:41,459 --> 00:17:45,900
And rightly so, since what was required, above all for political purposes, were

256
00:17:45,900 --> 00:17:49,436
short-clipped words of unmistakable meaning which could be uttered rapidly

257
00:17:49,436 --> 00:17:53,580
and which roused the minimum of echoes in the speaker's mind.

258
00:17:53,580 --> 00:17:57,120
The words of the B vocabulary even gained in force from the fact that

259
00:17:57,120 --> 00:17:59,339
nearly all of them were very much alike.

260
00:17:59,339 --> 00:18:05,756
Almost invariably those words good think mini packs Pro feed sex crime Joy Camp

261
00:18:05,756 --> 00:18:10,620
insock belly feel thinkpole and countless others— —were words of two or

262
00:18:10,620 --> 00:18:15,415
three syllables, with the stress distributed equally between the first syllable and the last.

263
00:18:15,415 --> 00:18:20,699
The use of them encouraged a gabbling style of speech, at once staccato and monotonous.

264
00:18:20,699 --> 00:18:22,500
And this was exactly what was aimed at.

265
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:26,756
The intention was to make speech, and especially speech on any subject not

266
00:18:26,756 --> 00:18:31,739
ideologically neutral, as nearly as possible independent of consciousness.

267
00:18:31,739 --> 00:18:35,279
For the purposes of everyday life it was no doubt necessary, or sometimes

268
00:18:35,279 --> 00:18:39,660
necessary, to reflect before speaking, but a Party member called upon to make a

269
00:18:39,660 --> 00:18:43,620
political or ethical judgment should be able to spray forth the correct opinions

270
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:47,095
as automatically as a machine gun spraying forth bullets.

271
00:18:47,095 --> 00:18:50,756
His training fitted him to do this, the language gave him an almost foolproof

272
00:18:50,756 --> 00:18:54,660
instrument, and the texture of the words, with the harsh sound and a certain

273
00:18:54,660 --> 00:18:58,860
willful ugliness which was in accord with the spirit of ingzock assisted the

274
00:18:58,860 --> 00:19:00,959
process still further.

275
00:19:00,959 --> 00:19:04,500
So did the fact of having very few words to choose from.

276
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:08,819
Relative to our own, the Newspeak vocabulary was tiny, and new ways of

277
00:19:08,819 --> 00:19:11,275
reducing it were constantly being devised.

278
00:19:11,275 --> 00:19:15,239
Newspeak, indeed, differed from most all other languages in that its vocabulary

279
00:19:15,239 --> 00:19:17,819
grew smaller instead of larger every year.

280
00:19:17,819 --> 00:19:22,379
Each reduction was again since the smaller the area of choice, the

281
00:19:22,379 --> 00:19:24,660
smaller the temptation to take thought.

282
00:19:24,660 --> 00:19:28,139
Ultimately it was hoped to make articulate speech issue from the larynx

283
00:19:28,139 --> 00:19:31,080
without involving the higher brain centers at all.

284
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:36,775
This aim was frankly admitted in the Newspeak word duck speak meaning 'to quack like a duck'.

285
00:19:36,775 --> 00:19:41,519
Like various other words in the B vocabulary duck speak was ambivalent in meaning.

286
00:19:41,519 --> 00:19:44,879
Provided that the opinions which were cracked out were orthodox ones, it

287
00:19:44,879 --> 00:19:48,360
implied nothing but praise, and when the Times referred to one of

288
00:19:48,360 --> 00:19:51,480
the orators of the Party as a double-plus good duck speaker it was

289
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:54,540
paying a warm and valued compliment.

290
00:19:54,540 --> 00:19:56,639
The C vocabulary.

291
00:19:56,639 --> 00:20:00,415
The C vocabulary was supplementary to the others and consisted entirely of

292
00:20:00,415 --> 00:20:02,580
scientific and technical terms.

293
00:20:02,580 --> 00:20:06,775
These resembled the scientific terms in use today, and were constructed from the

294
00:20:06,775 --> 00:20:10,620
same roots, but the usual care was taken to define them rigidly and strip them of

295
00:20:10,620 --> 00:20:14,756
undesirable meanings. They followed the same grammatical rules as the words in

296
00:20:14,756 --> 00:20:16,620
the other two vocabularies.

297
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:21,540
Very few of the C words had any currency either in everyday speech or in political speech

298
00:20:21,540 --> 00:20:24,900
in his scientific worker or technician could find all the words he needed in

299
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:28,620
the list devoted to his own specialty but he seldom had more than the

300
00:20:28,620 --> 00:20:31,680
dismantling of the words occurring in the other lists.

301
00:20:31,680 --> 00:20:35,699
Only a very few words were common to all lists, and there was no vocabulary

302
00:20:35,699 --> 00:20:39,500
expressing the function of Science as a habit of mind, or a method of thought,

303
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:42,660
irrespective of its particular branches.

304
00:20:42,660 --> 00:20:46,860
There was, indeed, no word for 'Science', any meaning that it could possibly bear

305
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:52,500
being already sufficiently covered by the word insock From the foregoing account it will be

306
00:20:52,500 --> 00:20:56,275
seen that in Newspeak the expression of unorthodox opinions, above a very low

307
00:20:56,275 --> 00:20:58,915
level, was well now impossible.

308
00:20:58,915 --> 00:21:04,019
It was of course possible to utter heresies of a very crude kind, a species of blasphemy.

309
00:21:04,019 --> 00:21:07,860
It would have been possible, for example, to say Big Brother is ungood.

310
00:21:07,860 --> 00:21:11,639
But this statement, which to an orthodox here merely conveyed a self-evident

311
00:21:11,639 --> 00:21:15,720
absurdity, could not have been sustained by reasoned argument, because the

312
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:17,936
necessary words were not available.

313
00:21:17,936 --> 00:21:21,900
Ideas inimical to wingstock could only be entertained in a vague wordless form,

314
00:21:21,900 --> 00:21:25,319
and could only be named in very broad terms which lumped together and

315
00:21:25,319 --> 00:21:29,879
condemned whole groups of heresies without defining them in doing so.

316
00:21:29,879 --> 00:21:33,900
One could, in fact, only use Newspeak for unorthodox purposes by illegitimately

317
00:21:33,900 --> 00:21:39,180
translating some of the words back into world speak For example, All mans are equal was a

318
00:21:39,180 --> 00:21:43,256
possible Newspeak sentence, but only in the same sense in which All men are

319
00:21:43,256 --> 00:21:45,959
RedHead is a possible old speak sentence.

320
00:21:45,959 --> 00:21:50,819
It did not contain a grammatical error, but it expressed a palpable untruth that

321
00:21:50,819 --> 00:21:54,595
is that all men are of equal size, weight or strength.

322
00:21:54,595 --> 00:21:58,319
The concept of political equality no longer existed, and this secondary

323
00:21:58,319 --> 00:22:01,256
meaning had accordingly been purged out of the word equal.

324
00:22:01,256 --> 00:22:06,059
In 1984, when old speak was still the normal means of communication, the danger

325
00:22:06,059 --> 00:22:11,095
theoretically existed that in using Newspeak words one might remember their original meanings.

326
00:22:11,095 --> 00:22:14,400
In practice it was not difficult for any person well grounded in a double thing

327
00:22:14,400 --> 00:22:18,000
to avoid doing this, but within a couple of generations even

328
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,819
the possibility of such elapse would have vanished.

329
00:22:20,819 --> 00:22:26,095
A person growing up with news speakers his soul language would no more know that equal had once

330
00:22:26,095 --> 00:22:29,639
had the secondary meaning of 'politically equal', or that free had once met

331
00:22:29,639 --> 00:22:33,540
intellectually free then for instance, a person who had never heard of chess

332
00:22:33,540 --> 00:22:37,500
would be aware of the secondary meanings attaching to queen and rook.

333
00:22:37,500 --> 00:22:40,860
There would be many crimes and errors which it would be beyond his power to

334
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:44,580
commit, simply because they were nameless and therefore unimaginable.

335
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:48,000
And it was to be foreseen that with the passage of time the distinguishing

336
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,080
characteristics of Newspeak would become more and more pronounced— —its words

337
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:56,459
growing fewer and fewer, their meanings more and more rigid, and the chance of

338
00:22:56,459 --> 00:23:00,059
putting them to improper use always diminishing.

339
00:23:00,059 --> 00:23:04,080
When old speak had been once and for all superseded, the last link with the past

340
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:05,639
would have been severed.

341
00:23:05,639 --> 00:23:09,300
History had already been rewritten, but fragments of the literature of the past

342
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:13,559
survived here and there, imperfectly censored, and so long as one retained

343
00:23:13,559 --> 00:23:16,620
one's knowledge of old speaker it was possible to read them.

344
00:23:16,620 --> 00:23:20,699
In the future such fragments, even if their chance to survive, would be

345
00:23:20,699 --> 00:23:23,040
unintelligible and untranslatable.

346
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:26,275
It was impossible to translate any passage of old speaker to Newspeak

347
00:23:26,275 --> 00:23:29,936
unless it either referred to some technical process or some very simple

348
00:23:29,936 --> 00:23:34,256
everyday action, or was already orthodox Good thankful would be the new speak

349
00:23:34,256 --> 00:23:36,595
expression) in tendency.

350
00:23:36,595 --> 00:23:40,559
In practice this meant that no book written before approximately 1960 could

351
00:23:40,559 --> 00:23:42,139
be translated as a whole.

352
00:23:42,139 --> 00:23:45,900
Pre-revolutionary literature could only be subjected to ideological translation—

353
00:23:45,900 --> 00:23:48,959
—that is, alteration incense as well as language.

354
00:23:48,959 --> 00:23:54,059
Take for example the well-known passage from the Declaration of Independence:

355
00:23:54,059 --> 00:23:58,256
We hold these truths to be self-evident, that all men are created equal, that they

356
00:23:58,256 --> 00:24:02,040
are endowed by their Creator with certain inalienable Rights, that among

357
00:24:02,040 --> 00:24:06,239
these are Life, Liberty and the pursuit of Happiness. That to secure these rights,

358
00:24:06,239 --> 00:24:11,580
Governments are instituted among Men, deriving their powers from the consent of the governed.

359
00:24:11,580 --> 00:24:15,720
That whenever any Form of Government becomes destructive of those ends, it is

360
00:24:15,720 --> 00:24:21,080
the Right of the People to alter or abolish it, and to institute new Government....

361
00:24:21,080 --> 00:24:24,775
It would have been quite impossible to render this into Newspeak while keeping

362
00:24:24,775 --> 00:24:26,275
to the sense of the original.

363
00:24:26,275 --> 00:24:29,819
The nearest one could come to doing so would be to swallow the whole passage up

364
00:24:29,819 --> 00:24:35,339
in the single word crime think A full translation could only be an ideological translation,

365
00:24:35,339 --> 00:24:41,300
whereby Jefferson's words would be changed into a panaderic on absolute government.

366
00:24:41,459 --> 00:24:46,739
A good deal of the literature of the past was, indeed, already being transformed in this way.

367
00:24:46,739 --> 00:24:50,339
Considerations of prestige made it desirable to preserve the memory of

368
00:24:50,339 --> 00:24:53,756
certain historical figures, while at the same time bringing their achievements

369
00:24:53,756 --> 00:24:58,080
into line with the philosophy of insock Various writers, such as Shakespeare,

370
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:03,415
Milton, Swift, Byron, Dickens and some others were therefore in process of translation:

371
00:25:03,415 --> 00:25:06,959
when the task had been completed, their original writings, with all else that

372
00:25:06,959 --> 00:25:09,900
survived with the literature of the past, would be destroyed.

373
00:25:09,900 --> 00:25:13,860
These translations were a slow and a difficult business, and it was not

374
00:25:13,860 --> 00:25:18,720
expected that they would be finished before the first or second decade of the 21st century.

375
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:22,040
There were also large quantities of merely utilitarian literature—

376
00:25:22,040 --> 00:25:27,120
—indispensable technical manuals, and the like— —that had to be treated in the same way.

377
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:30,720
It was chiefly in order to allow time for the preliminary work of translation

378
00:25:30,720 --> 00:25:36,660
that the final adoption of Newspeak had been fixed for so later date as 2050.

379
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:40,000
end of 1984 by George Orwell
